add("exoplanets","Exoplanets",["exoplanet","exoplanets","planets around other stars","how many exoplanets","alien planets","extrasolar planet"],"Exoplanets are planets around other stars. The NASA Exoplanet Archive listed 6,445 confirmed ones when this map's data was downloaded (14 Oct 2026). The map shows 6,417 of them (those with a known distance) around about 4,814 stars. Enter the Milky Way to see them.",EA,"concept","mwfield")
add("habzone","Habitable zone",["habitable zone","goldilocks zone","goldilocks","habitable"],"The habitable zone is the range of distances from a star where a planet could have liquid water on its surface: not too hot, not too cold. Being in it doesn't mean a planet has life (or even water).","NASA Exoplanet Exploration: Habitable zone","concept")
add("transit","How planets are found",["transit method","radial velocity","how are exoplanets found","how do you find planets","wobble method","direct imaging"],"Most exoplanets are found by the transit method (a planet dims its star as it passes in front, used by Kepler and TESS) or by radial velocity (the star wobbles as the planet tugs it). A few are photographed directly.","NASA Exoplanet Exploration: Discovery methods","concept")
add("life","Alien life?",["is there life","alien life","life on other planets","are we alone","extraterrestrial life","aliens exist","do aliens exist"],"Honest answer from a cartoon alien: no life has been found beyond Earth yet. Scientists are looking: Mars rovers, missions to icy moons like Europa and Enceladus, and JWST studying exoplanet atmospheres.","NASA Astrobiology","concept")
add("jwst","James Webb Space Telescope",["jwst","james webb","webb telescope","webb"],"JWST launched on 25 December 2021 and orbits the Sun near the L2 point, 1.5 million km from Earth. Its 6.5-metre gold mirror sees infrared light from the first galaxies and from exoplanet atmospheres.","NASA JWST mission pages","concept")
add("hubble","Hubble Space Telescope",["hubble telescope","hubble space telescope","hst"],"The Hubble Space Telescope launched in 1990 and orbits about 540 km above Earth. It helped measure the expansion rate and age of the universe.","NASA Hubble mission pages","concept")
add("kepler","Kepler mission",["kepler","kepler mission","kepler telescope","kepler space telescope","tess"],"NASA's Kepler telescope (2009-2018) stared at stars to catch planets transiting, and found most of the known exoplanets. TESS (since 2018) does the same for bright nearby stars across the whole sky.","NASA Kepler and TESS mission pages","concept")
