add("galaxy","Galaxies",["galaxy","galaxies","what is a galaxy","how many galaxies","types of galaxies","spiral galaxy","elliptical galaxy"],"A galaxy is a huge system of stars, gas, dust and dark matter held together by gravity. They come as spirals, ellipticals and irregulars. Estimates of how many there are in the observable universe range from a few hundred billion up to about 2 trillion.","NASA Science: Galaxies; Conselice et al. 2016, ApJ","concept")
add("mapcount","Galaxies on this map",["how many galaxies on the map","724000","724 000","dots","what are the dots","every dot"],"Every dot is a real galaxy: about 724,000 from three surveys (2MRS 43,105; SDSS DR17 183,012; DESI DR1 497,614). The map shows 100,000 by default; Advanced lets you load all of them.","Universe Map data sources (Huchra et al. 2012; SDSS DR17; DESI DR1)","map","home")
# ---------- clusters and big structures
def lab(name):return [s for s in data['T'] if s['name']==name][0]
add("virgo","Virgo Cluster",["virgo cluster","virgo","virgo cluster of galaxies"],"The Virgo Cluster is the nearest big galaxy cluster, with over 1,000 (up to about 2,000) member galaxies; M87 sits at its heart. Standard distance: about 54 million light-years (16.5 Mpc). The map's redshift-based distance is larger (about 62 million ly) because Virgo's galaxies move fast on their own, which skews redshift distances.","Mei et al. 2007, ApJ (16.5 Mpc); Binggeli et al. 1985; map: redshift with Planck 2018","cluster","lab:Virgo Cluster",{"dist":"The Virgo Cluster is about 54 million light-years away (16.5 Mpc). The map shows about 62 million ly from its redshift."},{"std":"54 million ly","map":"62 million ly"})
add("coma","Coma Cluster",["coma cluster","coma","abell 1656"],"The Coma Cluster has over 1,000 galaxies. In 1933 Fritz Zwicky noticed its galaxies move too fast to be held together by visible matter: an early clue to dark matter. Its light left before the dinosaurs existed.","Zwicky 1933; Carter et al. 2008; map: redshift with Planck 2018","cluster","lab:Coma Cluster",{"dist":"The Coma Cluster is about 330 million light-years away (about 100 Mpc), so its light left before the dinosaurs existed."},{"std":"~320-330 million ly","map":"332 million ly"})
add("perseus","Perseus Cluster",["perseus cluster","abell 426","perseus"],"The Perseus Cluster is one of the most massive nearby clusters. NASA's Chandra found pressure waves in its hot gas: the 'sound' of a black hole, a B-flat 57 octaves below middle C.","NASA Chandra 2003 (Fabian et al.); map: redshift with Planck 2018","cluster","lab:Perseus Cluster",{"dist":f"The map puts the Perseus Cluster at about {lab('Perseus Cluster')['ly']/1e6:.0f} million light-years (from redshift)."},{"map":f"{lab('Perseus Cluster')['ly']/1e6:.0f} million ly"})
gal("Abell S 373","fornaxcl",["fornax cluster","abell s 373"],"The Fornax Cluster is the second-richest cluster within about 100 million light-years, after Virgo. It's compact, in the southern sky.","about 62 million light-years (19 Mpc)","Blakeslee et al. 2009, ApJ")
gal("Abell 3526","centauruscl",["centaurus cluster","abell 3526"],"The Centaurus Cluster is a big southern galaxy cluster, part of the Hydra-Centaurus supercluster region.")
gal("Hydra I Cluster","hydra",["hydra cluster","hydra i","hydra i cluster"],"Hydra I is one of the biggest galaxy clusters within about 200 million light-years.")
gal("Abell 3627","norma",["norma cluster","abell 3627"],"The Norma Cluster is a massive cluster near the Great Attractor, partly hidden behind the Milky Way's dust.")
gal("Abell 2151","hercules",["hercules cluster","abell 2151"],"The Hercules Cluster is a cluster rich in spiral and merging galaxies.")
gal("Abell 2029","a2029",["abell 2029","ic 1101","biggest galaxy","largest galaxy"],"Abell 2029 is a huge cluster whose central galaxy, IC 1101, is one of the largest galaxies known.")
add("cluster","Galaxy clusters",["galaxy cluster","galaxy clusters","what is a cluster","cluster of galaxies"],"A galaxy cluster is hundreds to thousands of galaxies bound together by gravity, swimming in hot X-ray gas. Most of a cluster's mass is dark matter. They're the biggest gravitationally bound things in the universe.","NASA Chandra: Galaxy clusters","concept")
gal("Great Attractor (Norma Cluster, Abell 3627)","greatattractor",["great attractor","attractor"],"The Great Attractor is a huge concentration of mass that the Milky Way and thousands of galaxies are drifting towards, near the Norma Cluster, hidden behind the Milky Way's dusty disc. It's roughly at the heart of our home supercluster, Laniakea.","roughly 150 to 250 million light-years","Dressler et al. 1987; Kraan-Korteweg et al. 1996; Tully et al. 2014")
add("laniakea","Laniakea Supercluster",["laniakea","laniakea supercluster","our supercluster","home supercluster","immeasurable heaven"],"Laniakea ('immeasurable heaven' in Hawaiian) is our home supercluster, mapped by how galaxies flow (Tully et al. 2014). It's about 520 million light-years across, with about 100,000 galaxies and 10^17 Suns' worth of mass. The Milky Way is out near its edge.","Tully, Courtois, Hoffman & Pomarède 2014, Nature 513, 71","structure","gi:Great Attractor (Norma Cluster, Abell 3627)")
gal("Shapley Supercluster","shapley",["shapley supercluster","shapley","shapley concentration"],"The Shapley Supercluster is the biggest concentration of galaxies in our cosmic neighbourhood, with thousands of galaxies. It also tugs on the Local Group.",None,"Raychaudhury 1989; Proust et al. 2006")
