add("sloan","Sloan Great Wall",["sloan great wall","sloan wall","great wall","biggest structure"],"The Sloan Great Wall, found in SDSS data (Gott et al. 2005), is a giant wall of galaxies about 1.4 billion light-years long. It's about 1 billion light-years away; the map's approximate position is 1.1 billion ly.","Gott et al. 2005, ApJ 624, 463","structure","lab:Sloan Great Wall (approx.)",{"dist":"The Sloan Great Wall is about 1 billion light-years away (map: about 1.1 billion ly)."},{"std":"~1 billion ly","map":"1.1 billion ly"})
add("cfa2","CfA2 Great Wall",["cfa2 great wall","cfa2","cfa great wall","geller huchra"],"The CfA2 Great Wall (Geller & Huchra, 1989) was the first giant wall of galaxies found: about 500 million light-years long. It contains the Coma Cluster.","Geller & Huchra 1989, Science 246, 897","structure","lab:CfA2 Great Wall (approx.)")
gal("Sculptor Wall (approx.)","sclwall",["sculptor wall"],"The Sculptor Wall is a long sheet of galaxies in the southern sky.")
gal("Boötes Void","bootes",["bootes void","bootes","great nothing","boötes void","big void"],"The Boötes Void is one of the biggest known voids, about 330 million light-years across with only about 60 known galaxies inside (Kirshner et al. 1981). If the Milky Way were in it, we wouldn't have known other galaxies existed until the 1960s.","roughly 700 million light-years","Kirshner et al. 1981, ApJL; Kirshner et al. 1987")
add("void","Cosmic voids",["void","voids","cosmic void","empty space"],"Voids are huge, nearly empty bubbles between the walls and filaments of the cosmic web, typically tens to hundreds of millions of light-years across. They fill most of the universe's volume.","van de Weygaert & Platen 2011; Pan et al. 2012 (SDSS voids)","concept","gi:Boötes Void")
add("filament","Cosmic filaments",["filament","filaments","cosmic filament","galaxy filament"],"Filaments are long threads of galaxies and gas, up to hundreds of millions of light-years long, joining galaxy clusters like strands of a spider's web. Galaxies flow along them towards the clusters.","NASA; Tempel et al. 2014 (SDSS filaments)","concept","home")
add("cosmicweb","The cosmic web",["cosmic web","web","large scale structure","structure of the universe"],"On the biggest scales galaxies form a cosmic web: clusters at the knots, filaments and walls between them, and giant voids in the gaps. Dark matter's gravity built it from tiny ripples in the early universe. Every dot on this map is a real galaxy tracing it.","NASA; Springel et al. 2005 (Millennium simulation); Planck 2018","concept","home")
add("supercluster","Superclusters",["supercluster","superclusters","what is a supercluster"],"Superclusters are huge groupings of galaxy groups and clusters, hundreds of millions of light-years across. Unlike clusters, they're not held together by gravity: cosmic expansion will slowly pull them apart.","Tully et al. 2014, Nature","concept","gi:Shapley Supercluster")
add("wall","Galaxy walls",["galaxy wall","walls","sheet of galaxies"],"Walls are giant flat sheets of galaxies around voids, like the CfA2 Great Wall and the Sloan Great Wall.","Geller & Huchra 1989; Gott et al. 2005","concept","lab:CfA2 Great Wall (approx.)")
add("group","Galaxy groups",["galaxy group","group of galaxies"],"Galaxy groups are smaller than clusters: up to about 50 galaxies. The Milky Way's is the Local Group.","NASA","concept","lg")
# ---------- cosmology concepts
add("lightyear","Light-year",["light year","lightyear","light years","ly","what is a light year"],"A light-year is a distance, not a time: how far light travels in a year, about 9.46 trillion km. Light from the Moon takes 1.3 seconds, from the Sun 8 minutes 20 seconds.","IAU (1 ly = 9.4607 × 10^15 m)","concept")
add("parsec","Parsec",["parsec","parsecs","pc","kpc","kiloparsec","light years vs parsecs","parsec vs light year"],"A parsec (pc) is 3.26 light-years: the distance at which a star shifts by 1 arcsecond as Earth goes round the Sun. Astronomers love it. 1 kpc = 1,000 pc; 1 Mpc = 1 million pc.","IAU Resolution B2 (2015)","concept")
add("mpc","Megaparsec",["megaparsec","mpc","megaparsecs"],"1 megaparsec (Mpc) is 1 million parsecs, or 3.26 million light-years, about 3.1 × 10^19 km. This map's distances are in Mpc.","IAU","concept")
add("redshift","Redshift",["redshift","red shift","z","what is z","doppler"],"Redshift (z) is how much a galaxy's light has been stretched to redder wavelengths by the expansion of the universe. Bigger z means farther away and further back in time; z = 0.1 is about 1.4 billion light-years. This map turns redshifts into 3D positions.","Hubble 1929; Planck 2018 cosmology (as used on this map)","concept")
add("hubblelaw","Expanding universe",["expanding universe","expansion","hubble law","hubble constant","hubble tension","is the universe expanding"],"The universe is expanding: the farther a galaxy, the faster it moves away (Hubble's law). The rate, the Hubble constant, is about 67-73 km/s per megaparsec. Different methods disagree slightly, which is called the 'Hubble tension'.","Planck 2018 (67.4); Riess et al. 2022 (73.0)","concept")
add("age","Age of the universe",["age of the universe","how old is the universe","universe age","how old"],"The universe is about 13.8 billion years old (13.787 ± 0.020 billion, Planck 2018). The most distant galaxies on this map are seen as they were about 9.6 billion years ago.","Planck Collaboration 2020, A&A 641, A6","concept")
add("bigbang","The Big Bang",["big bang","beginning of the universe","how did the universe begin"],"The Big Bang wasn't an explosion in space: it was space itself starting hot and dense and expanding everywhere at once, about 13.8 billion years ago.","NASA WMAP; Planck 2018","concept")
add("cmb","Cosmic microwave background",["cmb","cosmic microwave background","microwave background","afterglow"],"The cosmic microwave background is the Big Bang's afterglow, released about 380,000 years after it began, when the universe cooled enough to turn see-through. Today it's a cold 2.725 K glow from every direction.","Fixsen 2009, ApJ (2.725 K); Planck 2018","concept")
add("darkmatter","Dark matter",["dark matter","what is dark matter","missing mass"],"Dark matter is invisible stuff that doesn't shine or absorb light but has gravity. There's about five times more of it than normal matter. We see its pull on galaxies and clusters (Zwicky 1933, Rubin 1970s), but nobody knows what it is yet.","Planck 2018 (~27% of the universe); Rubin & Ford 1970","concept")
add("darkenergy","Dark energy",["dark energy","accelerating universe","what is dark energy"],"Dark energy is the mystery making the universe's expansion speed up, discovered in 1998 with supernovae (Nobel Prize 2011). It's about 68% of the universe's energy. DESI's 2024-2025 results hint it might change over time.","Riess et al. 1998; Perlmutter et al. 1999; Planck 2018; DESI Collaboration 2025","concept")
add("composition","What the universe is made of",["what is the universe made of","universe made of","ordinary matter","normal matter","atoms"],"About 5% of the universe is ordinary matter (atoms: you, stars, planets), about 27% dark matter, and about 68% dark energy.","Planck Collaboration 2020, A&A 641, A6","concept")
add("observable","Observable universe",["universe","the universe","observable universe","size of the universe","how big is the universe","edge of the universe","end of the universe"],"The observable universe is about 93 billion light-years across (a radius of about 46 billion ly), bigger than 13.8 billion because space expanded while the light travelled. What lies beyond, we can't see. Nobody knows if the whole universe is infinite.","Planck 2018 cosmology; Gott et al. 2005","concept","home")
add("lightspeed","Speed of light",["speed of light","light speed","how fast is light","c"],"Light travels at exactly 299,792,458 m/s, about 300,000 km per second. Nothing goes faster. It could zip around Earth about 7.5 times in one second.","SI definition (BIPM)","concept")
add("lookback","Lookback time",["lookback time","looking back in time","seeing the past","time travel","time slider"],"Looking far away means looking back in time: you see each galaxy as it was when its light left. The map's time slider uses this lookback time.","Planck 2018 cosmology (as used on this map)","concept")
add("comoving","Comoving distance",["comoving distance","comoving","proper distance","how are distances measured"],"The map shows comoving distance: where galaxies are now, with the universe's expansion taken out. It's worked out from each galaxy's redshift using the Planck 2018 model. Nearby galaxies (like Virgo) can be a bit off because of their own motions.","Planck Collaboration 2020 (cosmology as used on this map)","concept")
add("bao","Baryon acoustic oscillations",["bao","baryon acoustic oscillations","standard ruler"],"Baryon acoustic oscillations are frozen sound waves from the early universe. They left a preferred spacing between galaxies, about 490 million light-years (150 Mpc), that DESI uses as a cosmic ruler to measure dark energy.","Eisenstein et al. 2005; DESI Collaboration 2025","concept")
add("gravity","Gravity",["gravity","what is gravity"],"Gravity is the pull between masses. In Einstein's general relativity, mass bends space and time, and things move along the curves. It's what built the cosmic web.","NASA","concept")
